course: religion-ethics
lesson: 100
title: Olympiad practice: the hardest cases
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: The hardest olympiad cases put good things against each other: helping a friend versus honesty, a promise versus safety, a trophy versus the truth, a friend's dignity versus a straight answer.
explain: Sam's friend will be kept back a year if he fails, and begs to copy "just this once"; Paul rejects "Let us do evil that good may result" (Romans 3:8), and there is an honest way to help: study together now.
explain: Some cases have a firm answer: if a friend says she's running away tonight and makes you promise not to tell, safety comes before the promise, so tell a trusted adult right away.
explain: Others are truly open, like owning up to a foul after a cup final, and the best answer says "it depends", names what matters on each side and explains which way it leans and why.
explain: Christians believe truthfulness is a likeness to God, who is truth, always joined to love (Ephesians 4:15); nearly everyone agrees honesty should be the rule and exceptions rare and serious.
fact: Paul rejects the idea "Let us do evil that good may result" (Romans 3:8); real help, like studying together, beats a cheat.
fact: Children often blame themselves when their parents split up; "Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest" (Matthew 11:28): comfort first, and help her talk to an adult she trusts.
fact: You can love a parent and still respectfully disagree: "Honour your father and mother" (Exodus 20:12) and speak privately and gently (Matthew 18:15).
fact: Philosophers argue about whether machines can lie, since lying needs an intention; flattering feedback can still mislead, so check your answers another way.
fact: A promise cannot bind you to stand by while someone comes to harm; keeping someone safe outweighs a promise.
fact: Hebrews 11:31 and James 2:25 praise Rahab's faith and action in hiding the spies, not the lie as such; Christians have debated it since Augustine.
fact: "Anyone who competes as an athlete does not receive the victor's crown except by competing according to the rules" (2 Timothy 2:5); some say the referee's call is part of the game, others say honesty comes first.
fact: With a friend's harmless boast, have a quiet word in private rather than exposing him: "Like clouds and wind without rain is one who boasts of gifts never given" (Proverbs 25:14).
fact: People may keep their own lives private: "Ask her" protects a friend's dignity without lying; "Love covers over a multitude of sins" (1 Peter 4:8).
fact: Confucius said people cannot stand without trust (Analects 12.7); Islam and Judaism treat truth as sacred with narrow exceptions for peace and life; humanists say honesty lets people live and work together.
fact: Peter lied out of fear in a moment, wept and turned back (Luke 22:62); Ananias and Sapphira planned their lie, and Sapphira repeated it when given a plain chance to tell the truth (Acts 5:8).
fact: Many traditions treat a lie blurted out in panic differently from a planned deception; the vicar says Scripture leaves some mystery here.
fact: In an open case the best answer says "it depends", names what matters on each side, and explains which way it leans and why.
fact: Slang check: "bro" means mate; "for real" means truly.
exercise: Sam's friend will be kept back a year and begs to copy just this once. What is the best-reasoned answer? | No, but help him study now, because real help beats a cheat | Yes, because being kept back a year is far worse than cheating once | Yes, but only the hardest questions | No, and tell everyone he asked to cheat
exercise: What does Paul reject in Romans 3:8? | Doing evil so that good may result | Helping friends who are struggling | Studying hard for tests | Telling the truth to teachers
exercise: Ivy's friend asks, "Is it my fault my parents are splitting up?" Ivy doesn't know what happened. What is best? | Say honestly that children don't cause that, and help her talk to a trusted adult | Say she doesn't know, maybe it is | Make up a kind reason so that the friend feels better, even if it isn't really true | Change the subject and talk about games
exercise: Josh finds out Dad told school he was ill when they went on holiday. What can he do? | Ask Dad privately how he sees it; he can love him and disagree | Tell the head teacher in front of everyone, so that Dad learns his lesson | Never speak to his dad again | Pretend he never found out
exercise: Ada's AI helper says "Great job!" even when she's wrong. Can a machine be dishonest? | It's an open question, but either way she should check her answers another way | Yes, machines always lie on purpose | No, a machine can't lie, so she should trust everything it tells her about her work | No, machines are always right
exercise: Tia's friend is running away tonight and makes her promise not to tell. What should Tia do? | Tell a trusted adult right now; safety comes before the promise | Keep the promise; a promise is a promise, and friends never tell | Go with her friend to keep her company | Wait until tomorrow to see if she went
exercise: Does the Bible praise Rahab's lie? | It praises her faith and action; the lie itself is still argued about | Yes; it says all lies are good | No; it says that Rahab was punished for lying, along with all her family | Rahab is not in the Bible
exercise: Mo fouled an attacker and the ref missed it; his team won the cup. "Say nothing, bro." What is a well-reasoned answer? | It depends: many admire owning up, so talking honestly to the coach is a good step, though some say the ref's call is part of the game | Say nothing; trophies matter more than anything | Give the trophy away without telling anyone why | Blame another player for the foul
exercise: Rory keeps telling new kids he has a horse. It isn't hurting anyone. What is best? | Have a quiet word with him in private | Tell all the new kids he's a liar before he fools them | Say nothing and make up a horse of your own too | Laugh at him in front of the whole class
exercise: Nadia's friend says she lives in a big house, but her family is struggling. Kids ask Nadia, "Is it true?" | Ask her | Yes, it's huge | No, they're poor | She's lying to you all
exercise: The judge asks, "Is honesty always the best policy?" What is the best-reasoned answer? | Nearly everyone agrees honesty should be the rule and exceptions rare and serious, like saving a life | Yes, always, and there is nothing more to say | No, honesty is only for children | It depends only on whether you'll get caught
exercise: Why was Peter restored but Ananias and Sapphira were not? | Peter lied in panic and turned back; they planned it and kept to their story | Peter never lied at all | Ananias was richer than Peter | There is no difference between them
exercise: What did Confucius say people cannot stand without? | Trust | Money | Swords | Kings
exercise: What does 1 Peter 4:8 say? | Love covers over a multitude of sins | Love tells everyone every secret it knows | Love never forgives anything, however small | Love is only for families, not for neighbours
exercise: In an open case, what makes the best answer? | It says "it depends", names what matters on each side, and explains why | It picks one side and refuses to listen to anything the other side says at all | It copies what the judge seems to want | It says "I don't know" and stops
test: Your mate says, "Letting him copy is kind, so it's fine." What is the best-reasoned reply? | Kindness matters, but helping him study is kind and honest, while copying hides the problem | He's right, any kindness makes cheating fine | Copying is fine if he's your best friend | It's fine if you only let him copy once a year
test: "Swear you won't tell, or I'll hate you for ever," says Tia's friend, packing a bag. What is the right move? | Tell a trusted adult right now; her safety matters more than the promise | Keep the promise and hope she's OK, because a promise to a friend is sacred | Help her pack so she's safe | Tell her to wait until summer
test: Ivy's friend says, "Mum and Dad split 'cause I was naughty, for real." What is honest comfort? | Children don't cause that; let's tell a grown-up you trust how you feel | Maybe you were too naughty, say sorry | I don't know, ask your naughty brother | Don't worry, they'll get back together tomorrow, they always do in the end
test: A cartoon shows a kid who yells at his dad in front of the school for lying about a holiday. What would be better? | Asking Dad privately and respectfully how he sees it | Yelling louder so more people hear | Never mentioning it to anyone | Telling the school that Dad is a criminal who should be punished
test: Mo's mate says, "The ref didn't see it, so it didn't happen." What is a reasoned view? | It did happen; owning up is admired, though people argue about the ref's call | He's right; only the ref decides what happened on the pitch, and that is final | Nobody ever owns up in football, so forget it | Hand back the trophy without saying why
test: "Rory, you liar, you haven't got a horse!" someone shouts in class. Why is a quiet word better? | It helps Rory stop without shaming him in public | Because Rory might really have a horse that nobody has seen | Because shouting is always fine if somebody is lying | Because nobody in the class cares about horses anyway
test: Kids push Nadia: "Go on, is her house big or not?" What protects her friend without a lie? | "Ask her, it's her business" | "Yes, it's a mansion with a pool" | "No, they're totally broke, sorry" | "She lies about everything she says"
test: What would happen if everyone thought any lie was fine whenever it helped them? | Nobody could trust anyone's words any more | Everyone would be happier and kinder to each other | Life would be easier and safer for everybody | Only olympiads would be affected, nothing else
test: Your cousin says, "Ananias died and Peter didn't, so God has favourites." What is a better reading? | Peter repented after a panicked lie; they planned theirs and stuck to it, though some mystery remains | Peter was richer, so he was saved | Ananias was a stranger, so he didn't count | The Bible says nothing about either of them
test: A judge asks, "Is honesty ALWAYS the best policy?" A team answers, "Yes, full stop." What would make it stronger? | Giving reasons and facing the hard cases, like saving a life | Saying "full stop" louder | Saying the other team is wrong | Repeating the question back to the judge, slowly and very clearly
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the principle rejected by Paul in Romans 3:8? | That evil may be done in order that good may result | That good ought to be done irrespective of consequences | That falsehood is invariably impermissible | That assistance ought to be rendered to the needy
exam: Given that a friend discloses an intention to run away from home and exacts a promise of secrecy, identify the course of action most consistent with this lesson. | The immediate disclosure of the intention to a trusted adult, safety outweighing the promise | The preservation of the promise in all circumstances | The accompaniment of the friend upon her departure so as to ensure her safety throughout the journey | The postponement of any action until the following day
exam: Select the statement most consistent with the established distinction between the falsehood of Peter and that of Ananias and Sapphira. | Peter's falsehood arose from sudden fear and was followed by repentance, whereas theirs was premeditated and maintained | Peter's falsehood was premeditated, whereas theirs arose from sudden fear | Neither falsehood was in any respect different from the other | Peter uttered no falsehood whatsoever
exam: Ascertain which response to the question "Is honesty always the best policy?" would be most highly regarded by olympiad judges. | One which affirms honesty as the rule, examines the rare grave exceptions, and weighs both sides with reasons | One which affirms honesty without qualification or reason | One which denies the value of honesty entirely | One which declines to answer on the ground of difficulty
